 **Parameters:**
-- **Scales:** Typically 3 scales (low, mid, high) [S1].
-- **Training Schedule:** Early training uses low-resolution voxels as pseudo-ground truth to guide coarse geometry learning. Medium- and high-resolution voxels gradually refine details as training advances [S1].
+- **Number of Scales ($L$):** Not given in the sources.
+- **Training Schedule:** Low-resolution voxels are prioritized early in training, with mid- and high-resolution voxels refining details as training advances [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Sparse Views:** In scenarios with significant viewpoint changes or sparse training views (e.g., 360° dataset), the model may face challenges in generalization [S1].
-- **Local Minima:** Omitting novel view rays risks local minima, causing incorrect geometry [S1].
-- **Pose Accuracy:** Few-shot NeRF relies on accurate camera poses for training [S1].
+- **Boundary Conditions:** If the reprojected pixel $\tilde{p}_{li \to j}$ falls outside the image bounds of frame $j$, the reprojection error is effectively infinite, and that scale is not selected [S1].
+- **Sparse Views:** The method relies on accurate camera poses. In scenarios with significant viewpoint changes or sparse training views (e.g., 360° dataset), generalization may be challenging [S1].
+- **Local Minima:** Omitting novel view rays risks local minima, causing incorrect geometry. Novel view regularizations add guidance, reducing overfitting [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Adds overhead for multi-scale rendering and reprojection calculations for each training ray. However, it eliminates the need for complex scheduling [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing multi-scale voxel grids and intermediate 3D points for all scales during the loss computation [S1].
-- **Performance:** Significantly reduces floaters and improves convergence quality across scales compared to training without geometric adaptation [S1]. Achieves lower final depth error than depth prior $L_d$ alone [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Self-adaptive, no complex scheduling required, robust to geometric noise compared to feature warping methods like GeCoNeRF [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires accurate camera poses; performance may degrade in extremely sparse view scenarios without novel view regularization [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Measured Performance:** On the FrugalNeRF framework, $L_{geo}$ alone achieves lower final depth error than the depth prior $L_d$ alone, which suffers from higher error later in training due to scale mismatches [S1]. Combining $L_{geo}$ and $L_d$ yields the best results [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Significantly reduces floaters and improves convergence quality across scales compared to training without geometric adaptation [S1]. Provides a stronger and more robust constraint than the depth prior ($L_d$) [S1]. Eliminates the need for complex scheduling and ground truth pose sampling [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Few-shot NeRF relies on accurate camera poses. Performance may decline on all metrics without the geometric adaptation loss $L_{geo}$ [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Cross-scale geometric adaptation was proposed in **FrugalNeRF** (2025) by the authors of [S1]. It was designed to accelerate and regularize training for extreme few-shot novel view synthesis without learned priors. The method leverages weight-sharing voxels across scales to capture varying scene frequencies and uses reprojection errors to guide multi-scale geometry [S1]. It addresses the limitations of standard depth priors which suffer from scale mismatches and methods like GeCoNeRF which are sensitive to geometric noise and produce floaters [S1].
+Cross-scale geometric adaptation was proposed in the FrugalNeRF framework, which synthesizes novel views from extremely few inputs [S1]. It was introduced to accelerate and regularize training without using learned priors [S1]. The method leverages weight-sharing voxels across scales to capture varying scene frequencies and uses reprojection errors to guide multi-scale geometry [S1]. It is compared against methods like GeCoNeRF, which uses warped features as pseudo labels and is sensitive to geometric noise, and ReVoRF, which uses unreliable regions for pseudo-novel view synthesis [S1].
 
